<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thomas Munro</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1761">1761</a>–<a href="/years/1827">1827</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sir Thomas Munro (1761–1827) was a Scottish soldier and administrator of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a>. He developed the ryotwari system, under which land revenue was settled directly with individual cultivators, and applied it as Governor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Madras</a> Presidency from 1820 until his death from cholera in 1827.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thomas-munro.jpg" alt="Thomas Munro" width="320" height="389" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Martin Archer Shee · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sir_Thomas_Munro,_1st_Bt_by_Sir_Martin_Archer_Shee.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Munro was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glasgow">Glasgow</a> on 27 May 1761, the son of the merchant Alexander Munro, whose family tobacco business was ruined when the trade collapsed during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolutionary-war">American Revolutionary War</a>. He was educated at the University of Glasgow, where he was known for his strength and as a boxer. He was meant to join his father's business, but in 1779 he took up an infantry cadetship in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Madras</a>. A claimed descent from George Munro, 10th Baron of Foulis, chief of Clan Munro, has been contested by the clan historian R. W. Munro.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He served in the war against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyder-ali">Hyder Ali</a> from 1780 to 1783 and in the first campaign against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tipu-sultan">Tipu Sultan</a> from 1790 to 1792. He was then one of four officers chosen to administer territory taken from Tipu, where he spent seven years learning revenue survey and assessment. After Tipu's final defeat in 1799 he restored order in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanara">Kanara</a>, and from 1800 to 1807 he ran the Ceded Districts given up by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizam-of-hyderabad">Nizam of Hyderabad</a>, where he introduced the ryotwari system.</p><p>On a long furlough in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> he gave evidence on the renewal of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a>'s charter, and in 1813 he told questioners that if agriculture, manufactures, schools, hospitality and respect towards women denote a civilised people, &quot;then the Hindoos are not inferior in civilization to the people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.&quot; He returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Madras</a> in 1814 to reform the judicial and police systems. In the Pindari War of 1817 he commanded the reserve division against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshwa">Peshwa</a>'s southern lands, where nine forts surrendered or were taken, and he was made a Knight Commander of the Order of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bath">Bath</a> in 1819.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>As governor he founded systems of revenue assessment and administration that largely lasted into the twentieth century, and his official minutes, published by Sir A. Arbuthnot, served later officials as a manual. He was created a baronet in 1825, and a committee of public instruction formed at his request in 1826 led in time to Presidency College. He died on tour in the Ceded Districts, where memorials keep his name, and Francis Chantrey's equestrian statue of him, riding without stirrups, stands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Chennai</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sir Thomas Munro, 1st Baronet” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the south <b>Thomas Munro</b>'s ryotwari system settled revenue directly with the peasants.</blockquote><a href="/empire/company-rule-in-india">Company rule in India · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Thomas Munro</b> introduced the ryotwari system, assessing land revenue directly on peasant cultivators.</blockquote><a href="/state/madras-presidency">Madras Presidency</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glasgow">Glasgow</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Pattikonda, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=55.86,-4.25&amp;year=1803" aria-label="Glasgow on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Thomas Munro"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4796 5347h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4796 5347h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=55.86,-4.25&amp;year=1803">What was here in 1803? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
